# ============================================================
# flow/keepout_blocks_partner_reach.buda
#
# A trunk placed where its PARTNER cannot reach it.  The
# REGRESSION VEHICLE for prune_unreachable_partner_windows
# (nuts.cpp) — it was an open defect when it was written, and
# the fix landed against it.  Sibling of
# flow/antenna_culled_partner.buda: same geometry, same cull,
# DIFFERENT cause —
#
#   antenna_culled_partner  keepout declared AFTER run_planner,
#                           so the pass ORDER strands metal
#                           (STILL OPEN)
#   this vehicle            keepout declared UP FRONT, and the
#                           PLACER walked into it  (FIXED)
#
# NUTS places each segment against keepouts ON ITS OWN LAYER.
# The trunk here is H on M4; the keepout is on M5.  So the trunk
# saw no obstacle, took the middle of its window, and the V stub
# that has to climb from it into `rcv` was left with no legal
# position at all.
#
# What the engine reports about that window:
#
#   seg1  H M4  along[190,710] perp=525  slide=[220..580]=360
#         pull=none(0)  opt=[200..600]  anchors=2/2
#
# `pull=none(0)` and a flat optimum spanning the whole window:
# moving the trunk trades one V stub's length for the other's,
# so EVERY position costs the same wirelength.  The placer took
# the centre — exactly 400 — and 400 is inside the keepout's
# y-band (380..450).  Only perp >= 450 keeps the partner clear.
#
# MEASURED (this vehicle, four ways):
#
#   before the fix, no healer   4 bits unplaced, 4 violations
#   before the fix + ripup      clean, detailed WL 4297
#   trunk hand-slid to [460,580] clean, detailed WL 3715
#   WITH the fix, no healer     clean, detailed WL 3715
#
# So the healer did rescue it — by RE-PINNING to a detour
# topology (V at perp=20, around the outside), not by nudging
# the trunk.  That is +15.7% detailed WL to buy back what a
# 50-unit slide inside a WL-FLAT window gives for nothing, and
# it is the expensive answer the prune exists to avoid needing.
#
# The prune narrows the seat window to [450..544] (544, not
# 580, because apply_interval_constraints' trunk margin already
# took 10% of the span) and NUTS then seats the trunk clear on
# its own.  It is conservative by construction: a zone only
# dooms a partner when the partner cannot SLIDE CLEAR of it, so
# the pass removes provably-dead positions or removes nothing.
#
# The vehicle now ends CLEAN, and that is the assertion.  Its
# guard is test/tests/test_partner_reach_prune.py, which pins
# the BOUNDARY in both directions — a guard asserting only
# "clean" would pass just as well if the prune threw the whole
# window away.  Do not add it to the QoR corpus (the corpus
# measures routing quality across designs; this measures one
# boundary, and the test is where that belongs).
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/keepout_blocks_partner_reach.buda --no-viz
# ============================================================
def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33
def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

# Up front, unlike the culled-partner twin: the planner sees it.
add_keepout 600 380 900 450 5
corner_margin dx 10 dy 10

add_block drv  100 100  200 200
add_block rcv  700 600  800 700
add_bus b[4] drv.tx rcv.rx

run_bundler strict
generate_topologies

# Candidate 4 is Z_VHV@y525@x190 — V up from drv, H across, V
# into rcv.  Pinned so the vehicle shows ONE shape; the pin is by
# index, so a change to candidate ORDER moves it.
select_topology 1 4

run_planner
run_nuts
run_detailed_nuts
dump_topologies --conn
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
